{"context_id":"ctx.more-algebra.definition-stably-free","kind":"definition","source_file":"more-algebra.tex","source_revision":"a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14","introduction_line":53,"source_end_line":64,"source_label":"definition-stably-free","source_url":"https://github.com/stacks/stacks-project/blob/a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14/more-algebra.tex#L53","source_excerpt":"\\begin{definition}\n\\label{definition-stably-free}\nLet $R$ be a ring. \n\\begin{enumerate}\n\\item Two modules $M$, $N$ over $R$ are said to be\n{\\it stably isomorphic} if there exist $n, m \\geq 0$ such\nthat $M \\oplus R^{\\oplus m} \\cong N \\oplus R^{\\oplus n}$\nas $R$-modules.\n\\item A module $M$ is {\\it stably free} if it is stably isomorphic\nto a free module.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\\end{definition}","scope_introduction_evidence":"\\begin{definition}","scope_kind":"chapter_terminology_available_after_definition","scope_end_line":10793,"is_standing_assumption":false,"candidate_ids_using":[]}
{"context_id":"ctx.more-algebra.artin-rees-c-works","kind":"terminology","source_file":"more-algebra.tex","source_revision":"a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14","introduction_line":184,"source_end_line":192,"source_url":"https://github.com/stacks/stacks-project/blob/a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14/more-algebra.tex#L184","source_excerpt":"\\medskip\\noindent\nLet $A$ be a Noetherian ring and let $I \\subset A$ be an ideal. Given a\nhomomorphism $f : M \\to N$ of finite $A$-modules there exists a $c \\geq 0$\nsuch that\n$$\nf(M) \\cap I^nN \\subset f(I^{n - c}M)\n$$\nfor all $n \\geq c$, see Algebra, Lemma \\ref{algebra-lemma-map-AR}. In this\nsituation we will say {\\it $c$ works for $f$ in the Artin-Rees lemma}.","scope_introduction_evidence":"Artin-Rees c works for f is defined by an image intersection inclusion quantified for n>=c.","scope_kind":"source_usage_within_scoped_sections","scope_end_line":317,"is_standing_assumption":false,"candidate_ids_using":["candidate.stacks.88285baf0b3d0a90a4280607","candidate.stacks.a4cfcdf408b1d3989ae4717b"]}
{"context_id":"ctx.more-algebra.situation-module-over-fibre-product","kind":"named_situation","source_file":"more-algebra.tex","source_revision":"a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14","introduction_line":524,"source_end_line":541,"source_label":"situation-module-over-fibre-product","source_url":"https://github.com/stacks/stacks-project/blob/a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14/more-algebra.tex#L524","source_excerpt":"\\begin{situation}\n\\label{situation-module-over-fibre-product}\nIn the following we will consider ring maps\n$$\n\\xymatrix{\nB \\ar[r] & A & A' \\ar[l]\n}\n$$\nwhere we assume $A' \\to A$ is surjective with kernel $I$.\nIn this situation we set $B' = B \\times_A A'$ to\nobtain a cartesian square\n$$\n\\xymatrix{\nA & A' \\ar[l] \\\\\nB \\ar[u] & B' \\ar[l] \\ar[u]\n}\n$$\n\\end{situation}","scope_introduction_evidence":"\\begin{situation}","scope_kind":"explicit_named_reference_required","scope_end_line":10793,"is_standing_assumption":false,"candidate_ids_using":["candidate.stacks.c7101d8603ab86becbc31c78","candidate.stacks.4e4f387dbdad41128d33c2d3","candidate.stacks.c42cc77baaf1a77ed0f938c4","candidate.stacks.6fca359f9e70fe30d493c687","candidate.stacks.675048922055d1cb82dc7bfe"]}
{"context_id":"ctx.more-algebra.situation-relative-module-over-fibre-product","kind":"named_situation","source_file":"more-algebra.tex","source_revision":"a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14","introduction_line":952,"source_end_line":999,"source_label":"situation-relative-module-over-fibre-product","source_url":"https://github.com/stacks/stacks-project/blob/a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14/more-algebra.tex#L952","source_excerpt":"\\begin{situation}\n\\label{situation-relative-module-over-fibre-product}\nLet $A, A', B, B', I$ be as in\nSituation \\ref{situation-module-over-fibre-product}.\nLet $B' \\to D'$ be a ring map. Set $D = D' \\otimes_{B'} B$,\n$C' = D' \\otimes_{B'} A'$, and $C = D' \\otimes_{B'} A$. This leads\nto a big commutative diagram\n$$\n\\xymatrix{\nC & & & C' \\ar[lll] \\\\\n& A \\ar[ul] & A' \\ar[l] \\ar[ru] \\\\\n& B \\ar[u] \\ar[ld] & B' \\ar[l] \\ar[u] \\ar[rd] \\\\\nD \\ar[uuu] & & & D' \\ar[lll] \\ar[uuu]\n}\n$$\nof rings.\nObserve that we do {\\bf not} assume that the map $D' \\to D \\times_C C'$\nis an isomorphism\\footnote{But $D' \\to D \\times_C C'$ is surjective\nby Lemma \\ref{lemma-module-over-fibre-product-bis}.}.\nIn this situation we have the functor\n\\begin{equation}\n\\label{equation-relative-functor}\n\\text{Mod}_{D'} \\longrightarrow\n\\text{Mod}_D \\times_{\\text{Mod}_C} \\text{Mod}_{C'},\\quad\nL' \\longmapsto (L' \\otimes_{D'} D, L' \\otimes_{D'} C', can)\n\\end{equation}\nanalogous to (\\ref{equation-functor}). Note that\n$L' \\otimes_{D'} D = L \\otimes_{D'} (D' \\otimes_{B'} B) = L \\otimes_{B'} B$\nand similarly\n$L' \\otimes_{D'} C' = L \\otimes_{D'} (D' \\otimes_{B'} A') = L \\otimes_{B'} A'$\nhence the diagram\n$$\n\\xymatrix{\n\\text{Mod}_{D'} \\ar[r] \\ar[d] &\n\\text{Mod}_D \\times_{\\text{Mod}_C} \\text{Mod}_{C'} \\ar[d] \\\\\n\\text{Mod}_{B'} \\ar[r] &\n\\text{Mod}_B \\times_{\\text{Mod}_A} \\text{Mod}_{A'}\n}\n$$\nis commutative. In the following we will write $(N, M', \\varphi)$ for an\nobject of $\\text{Mod}_D \\times_{\\text{Mod}_C} \\text{Mod}_{C'}$,\ni.e., $N$ is a $D$-module, $M'$ is an $C'$-module\nand $\\varphi : N \\otimes_B A \\to M' \\otimes_{A'} A$ is an isomorphism\nof $C$-modules.\nHowever, it is often more convenient think of $\\varphi$ as a $D$-linear\nmap $\\varphi : N \\to M'/IM'$ which induces an isomorphism\n$N \\otimes_B A \\to M' \\otimes_{A'} A = M'/IM'$.\n\\end{situation}","scope_introduction_evidence":"\\begin{situation}","scope_kind":"explicit_named_reference_required","scope_end_line":10793,"is_standing_assumption":false,"candidate_ids_using":[]}
{"context_id":"ctx.more-algebra.definition-fitting-ideal","kind":"definition","source_file":"more-algebra.tex","source_revision":"a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14","introduction_line":1413,"source_end_line":1418,"source_label":"definition-fitting-ideal","source_url":"https://github.com/stacks/stacks-project/blob/a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14/more-algebra.tex#L1413","source_excerpt":"\\begin{definition}\n\\label{definition-fitting-ideal}\nLet $R$ be a ring. Let $M$ be a finite $R$-module. Let $k \\geq 0$.\nThe {\\it $k$th Fitting ideal} of $M$ is the ideal $\\text{Fit}_k(M)$\nconstructed in Lemma \\ref{lemma-fitting-ideal}. Set $\\text{Fit}_{-1}(M) = 0$.\n\\end{definition}","scope_introduction_evidence":"\\begin{definition}","scope_kind":"chapter_terminology_available_after_definition","scope_end_line":10793,"is_standing_assumption":false,"candidate_ids_using":[]}
{"context_id":"ctx.more-algebra.pair-morphism","kind":"terminology","source_file":"more-algebra.tex","source_revision":"a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14","introduction_line":2239,"source_end_line":2243,"source_url":"https://github.com/stacks/stacks-project/blob/a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14/more-algebra.tex#L2239","source_excerpt":"\\noindent\nIn this section and the next a {\\it pair} is a pair $(A, I)$ where $A$\nis a ring and $I \\subset A$ is an ideal. A {\\it morphism of pairs}\n$(A, I) \\to (B, J)$ is a ring map $\\varphi : A \\to B$ with\n$\\varphi(I) \\subset J$.","scope_introduction_evidence":"Pair (A,I) is ring and ideal; pair morphism preserves ideal inclusion.","scope_kind":"source_usage_within_scoped_sections","scope_end_line":2971,"is_standing_assumption":false,"candidate_ids_using":["candidate.stacks.b1e6b3ef9e57dc7f1e302cd2","candidate.stacks.41719c853681579a2e28853f","candidate.stacks.b31394020216ee1d9e957921","candidate.stacks.fc071624ceb964686f8f73e1","candidate.stacks.904b90423a2188197d70ba22","candidate.stacks.9147b2e5f8979a9d291e709b","candidate.stacks.5a4c1ae526e46107a54020df","candidate.stacks.226fd3a11c036d8fd2ad72bf","candidate.stacks.769cfada61871d2cfe8a12ce","candidate.stacks.076a588fc5640eeea5865610","candidate.stacks.fde801acf254b715b3035124","candidate.stacks.ae05b214dc24719a2905cf25","candidate.stacks.100deb4d584560048e2f1c9c","candidate.stacks.b3e44718f4983e656a9d6a72","candidate.stacks.b190ba5c25d88787a1ee4f22","candidate.stacks.17a6c49c5b5712e5e60aee39"]}
{"context_id":"ctx.more-algebra.definition-zariski-pair","kind":"definition","source_file":"more-algebra.tex","source_revision":"a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14","introduction_line":2245,"source_end_line":2249,"source_label":"definition-zariski-pair","source_url":"https://github.com/stacks/stacks-project/blob/a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14/more-algebra.tex#L2245","source_excerpt":"\\begin{definition}\n\\label{definition-zariski-pair}\nA {\\it Zariski pair} is a pair $(A, I)$ such that\n$I$ is contained in the Jacobson radical of $A$.\n\\end{definition}","scope_introduction_evidence":"\\begin{definition}","scope_kind":"chapter_terminology_available_after_definition","scope_end_line":10793,"is_standing_assumption":false,"candidate_ids_using":[]}
{"context_id":"ctx.more-algebra.bar-xi","kind":"notation","source_file":"more-algebra.tex","source_revision":"a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14","introduction_line":2384,"source_end_line":2390,"source_url":"https://github.com/stacks/stacks-project/blob/a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14/more-algebra.tex#L2384","source_excerpt":"Some of the results of Section \\ref{section-lifting} may be viewed as results\nabout henselian pairs. In this section a {\\it pair} is a pair $(A, I)$\nwhere $A$ is a ring and $I \\subset A$ is an ideal. A {\\it morphism of pairs}\n$(A, I) \\to (B, J)$ is a ring map $\\varphi : A \\to B$ with\n$\\varphi(I) \\subset J$. As in\nSection \\ref{section-lifting} given an object $\\xi$ over $A$ we denote\n$\\overline{\\xi}$ the ``base change'' of $\\xi$ to an object over $A/I$","scope_introduction_evidence":"Bar xi is reduction/base change of xi to A/I.","scope_kind":"source_usage_within_scoped_sections","scope_end_line":2971,"is_standing_assumption":false,"candidate_ids_using":["candidate.stacks.904b90423a2188197d70ba22","candidate.stacks.9147b2e5f8979a9d291e709b","candidate.stacks.5a4c1ae526e46107a54020df","candidate.stacks.226fd3a11c036d8fd2ad72bf","candidate.stacks.769cfada61871d2cfe8a12ce","candidate.stacks.076a588fc5640eeea5865610","candidate.stacks.fde801acf254b715b3035124","candidate.stacks.ae05b214dc24719a2905cf25","candidate.stacks.100deb4d584560048e2f1c9c","candidate.stacks.b3e44718f4983e656a9d6a72","candidate.stacks.b190ba5c25d88787a1ee4f22","candidate.stacks.17a6c49c5b5712e5e60aee39"]}
{"context_id":"ctx.more-algebra.definition-henselian-pair","kind":"definition","source_file":"more-algebra.tex","source_revision":"a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14","introduction_line":2393,"source_end_line":2404,"source_label":"definition-henselian-pair","source_url":"https://github.com/stacks/stacks-project/blob/a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14/more-algebra.tex#L2393","source_excerpt":"\\begin{definition}\n\\label{definition-henselian-pair}\nA {\\it henselian pair} is a pair $(A, I)$ satisfying\n\\begin{enumerate}\n\\item $I$ is contained in the Jacobson radical of $A$, and\n\\item for any monic polynomial $f \\in A[T]$ and factorization\n$\\overline{f} = g_0h_0$ with $g_0, h_0 \\in A/I[T]$ monic\ngenerating the unit ideal in $A/I[T]$, there\nexists a factorization $f = gh$ in $A[T]$ with $g, h$ monic\nand $g_0 = \\overline{g}$ and $h_0 = \\overline{h}$.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\\end{definition}","scope_introduction_evidence":"\\begin{definition}","scope_kind":"chapter_terminology_available_after_definition","scope_end_line":10793,"is_standing_assumption":false,"candidate_ids_using":[]}
{"context_id":"ctx.more-algebra.definition-absolutely-integrally-closed","kind":"definition","source_file":"more-algebra.tex","source_revision":"a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14","introduction_line":3509,"source_end_line":3513,"source_label":"definition-absolutely-integrally-closed","source_url":"https://github.com/stacks/stacks-project/blob/a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14/more-algebra.tex#L3509","source_excerpt":"\\begin{definition}\n\\label{definition-absolutely-integrally-closed}\nA ring $A$ is {\\it absolutely integrally closed} if every\nmonic $f \\in A[T]$ is a product of linear factors.\n\\end{definition}","scope_introduction_evidence":"\\begin{definition}","scope_kind":"chapter_terminology_available_after_definition","scope_end_line":10793,"is_standing_assumption":false,"candidate_ids_using":[]}
{"context_id":"ctx.more-algebra.definition-auto-ass","kind":"definition","source_file":"more-algebra.tex","source_revision":"a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14","introduction_line":3686,"source_end_line":3690,"source_label":"definition-auto-ass","source_url":"https://github.com/stacks/stacks-project/blob/a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14/more-algebra.tex#L3686","source_excerpt":"\\begin{definition}\n\\label{definition-auto-ass}\nA ring $R$ is said to be {\\it auto-associated} if $R$ is local and its\nmaximal ideal $\\mathfrak m$ is weakly associated to $R$.\n\\end{definition}","scope_introduction_evidence":"\\begin{definition}","scope_kind":"chapter_terminology_available_after_definition","scope_end_line":10793,"is_standing_assumption":false,"candidate_ids_using":[]}
